  "response": "Before the 1980s, the prevailing medical view held that stress and excess stomach acid caused peptic ulcers. This model shaped both theory and therapy. The stomach was regarded as too acidic to sustain bacterial life, and treatment centred on neutralizing or suppressing acid, on dietary restriction, and in refractory cases on surgery. Physicians saw the ulcer as a chronic condition of temperament and secretion. They did not see it as an infection, and this framework went largely unchallenged for decades.\n\nRobin Warren was a pathologist at Royal Perth Hospital in Western Australia. Despite the entrenched consensus attributing ulcers to stress and acid, Warren observed spiral-shaped bacteria in gastric biopsy specimens in 1979. He noted that these organisms appeared consistently in tissue showing inflammation. That observation sat uneasily with the assumption of a sterile stomach. By contrast with Warren's Perth posting, Marshall and Warren conducted their key research at a hospital in Melbourne. There the young clinician Barry Marshall joined the effort to characterize the organism and to determine whether it could be grown outside the body. Repeated attempts failed until a culture plate was left incubating over an extended holiday period. Their first successful culture of H. pylori, in 1982, came three years after those 1979 biopsy observations.\n\nCulturing the organism established that it existed as a distinct species, but it did not establish that the organism caused disease. Animal models were unsatisfactory, and the medical community remained sceptical. To test causation directly, Marshall deliberately ingested a culture of H. pylori in 1984. The result of that self-experiment admits exactly one of two descriptions, and the two cannot both be true. Either Marshall developed gastritis after ingesting the H. pylori culture, a gastritis that itself caused his decision to undertake the ingestion, or Marshall developed no gastric inflammation after ingesting the H. pylori culture. The endoscopist who examined Marshall's gastric biopsies determined that Marshall had in fact developed gastritis after ingesting the H. pylori culture. Whichever description holds, the episode concentrated attention on the bacterium. It also turned a contested histological finding into a question that could be tested experimentally in human subjects.\n\nThe decisive arguments came from clinical outcomes rather than from any single dramatic demonstration. Ulcers frequently recurred after treatment with acid-suppressing drugs alone. Patients whose symptoms had been controlled by antacids or H2-receptor antagonists often relapsed once medication stopped. That pattern suggested that acid was a proximate factor rather than the underlying cause. Building on this, clinical trials showed that eradicating H. pylori with antibiotics sharply reduced ulcer recurrence. Patients cured of the infection largely remained free of ulcers, which indicates that a substantial share of peptic ulcers is caused by H. pylori infection. The epidemiological picture is nevertheless not monocausal. At least one of two major causes, perhaps both, accounts for a given population's ulcer burden: H. pylori infection, or the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. The frequent recurrence of ulcers under acid suppression alone confirms that a substantial share of peptic ulcers is caused by the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Taken together, these findings displaced the stress-and-acid model and replaced it with a framework of identifiable, treatable causes. The tension between the old consensus and Warren's early observations was settled formally. The Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet determined that H. pylori plays a causal role in gastritis and peptic ulcer disease. That determination confirmed the bacterial account over the explanation that had dominated medicine for most of the twentieth century.\n\nRecognition followed this institutional endorsement. Marshall and Warren shared the 2005 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. In other words, the 2005 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was jointly awarded to Barry Marshall and Robin Warren. The award honoured a reorientation of gastroenterology that turned chronic management into curative antibiotic therapy. Whereas that prize belonged to the physiological sciences, Marshall and Warren received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for their discovery. Their work remains a standard case study in how persistent observation, reproducible culture, and controlled clinical evidence can overturn a long-held medical orthodoxy.",
